# Engineering standards

- Prefer the simplest design that satisfies the requirements.
- Preserve existing architecture unless there is a demonstrated reason to change it.
- Follow SOLID where it improves cohesion and reduces coupling; do not add abstractions speculatively.
- Avoid duplicated business logic, giant functions, giant components, and unrelated responsibilities.
- Do not introduce a dependency when existing code or platform features solve the problem adequately.
- Make the smallest coherent change; do not perform unrelated refactors.
- Document non-obvious decisions and important trade-offs, not self-explanatory code.
- Never claim tests or performance checks passed unless they were actually run.
- Report assumptions, unverified checks, and remaining risks.
  